  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Welcome, Maths Explorer!"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "This is a **check-in**, not a test. There are no wrong answers in the lesson, only chances to show what you already know. Take your time, use your fingers or draw if it helps, and ask a grown-up if you get stuck.\n\nToday we will visit five maths places: counting, adding, taking away, shapes and patterns."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "emoji": "🧮",
      "alt": "Let's explore maths!",
      "caption": "Let's explore maths!"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 1: Counting and numbers"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "Numbers are like a ladder. Each step up is **one more**. Counting in 2s, 5s and 10s helps us count big groups quickly."
    },
    {
      "type": "chart",
      "kind": "number_line",
      "title": "Each step up is one more",
      "caption": "3 is on the line. One more than 3 is 4.",
      "from": 0,
      "to": 10,
      "marks": [
        3,
        4
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "Count by 2s: 2, 4, 6, 8, ... what comes next?",
        "hint": "Add 2 to 8.",
        "answer": {
          "value": 10,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "multiple_choice",
        "prompt": "Which number is the biggest?",
        "hint": "Look at the tens first.",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "text": "19"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "text": "91"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "text": "29"
          }
        ],
        "answer": [
          "b"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "vocab",
      "word": "tens and ones",
      "meaning": "In the number 47, the 4 means 4 tens (40) and the 7 means 7 ones.",
      "example": "47 = 40 + 7"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 2: Adding"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "**Adding** means putting groups together. 3 apples and 2 apples make 5 apples. You can count on from the bigger number: for 6 + 3, start at 6 and count 7, 8, 9."
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "What is 6 + 3?",
        "answer": {
          "value": 9,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "number",
        "prompt": "What is 10 + 7?",
        "answer": {
          "value": 17,
          "tolerance": 0
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "activity",
      "title": "Counting treasure",
      "materials": [
        "10 small things such as coins, buttons or pasta"
      ],
      "instructions": [
        "Put the things in a pile and count them.",
        "Split them into two groups. How many in each?",
        "Say the adding sentence, for example 4 + 6 = 10.",
        "Try a different split and say the new sum."
      ]
    },
